{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass after these things.</b> After that accursed woman had accustomed all of them to speak of the righteous man, the Holy One, blessed be He, brought upon them the offense of these officers, so that they would turn to each other and not to him; and further, so that relief would come to the righteous man through them." "<b>The butler of the king of Egypt and the baker sinned.</b> Scripture does not explain their sin, but our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in the Midrash that the sin of the butler was that a fly was found in the cup, and the sin of the baker was that a pebble was found in the cake (גלוסקא) that he set before the king; therefore he was angry with both of them — the pebble in the cake was negligence on the baker's part, and therefore he was hanged; but the fly in the cup was not negligence on the butler's part, since after he had mixed the cup, at the moment he extended his hand to the king the fly fell into it; therefore he was delivered." "<b>To their lords, to the king of Egypt.</b> It should have said 'to their lords, to Pharaoh king of Egypt,' and then said simply afterward, ויקצוף על שני סריסיו — 'and he was wroth with his two officers.' But it may be explained that at the beginning of its mention here, Scripture tells from the start the end of what is to come: that his kingdom would depart from him because of Israel, and for this reason Scripture mentioned the kingdom by itself and the name by itself. So too you will find in the matter of his end at the Sea, that Scripture conducts itself in this same manner — as it is written (Exodus 14): ויוגד למלך מצרים כי ברח העם ויהפך לבב פרעה — 'and it was told to the king of Egypt that the people had fled, and the heart of Pharaoh turned' — when it should have said, 'and it was told to Pharaoh king of Egypt that the people had fled, and his heart turned'; but it mentioned the kingdom by itself and the name by itself, to teach of his end, for the day of his calamity was near, when the Holy One, blessed be He, would remove his kingdom from him and nothing would remain to him but the name alone. But in the matter of the hardening of his heart, it says ויחזק ה׳ את לב פרעה מלך מצרים — it mentioned his kingdom and his name together, to indicate that although Pharaoh is king of Egypt, the Holy One, blessed be He, is the great King over him, and He it is who strengthens his heart; and his heart is in His hand like streams of water."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:02.220166254Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}